WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.240
This is the airship Hindenburg, burning up
while trying to land in New Jersey

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.820
in 1937.

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:08.410
97 people were trapped inside.

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:09.860
It was a big deal at the time.

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:14.320
The Hindenburg was the greatest passenger
airship ever built.

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:17.630
Imagine something the size of an ocean liner,
but in the air.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:22.270
And it could make the journey from Europe
to the US in half the time.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:26.980
It was a shining example of the future of
commercial air travel.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:30.660
And all it took was a spark to bring the
whole thing down.

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:39.359
Air travel was on everyone’s minds in the
early 20th century.

00:00:39.359 --> 00:00:43.890
Aviators like Charles Lindbergh and Amelia
Earhart became huge celebrities after they

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.809
conquered the Atlantic in airplanes.

00:00:46.809 --> 00:00:49.739
But airships were something else entirely.

00:00:49.739 --> 00:00:54.170
Just two years after Lindbergh famously crossed
the Atlantic in a cramped monoplane,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.420
a giant German zeppelin flew around the
world,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:02.229
carrying paid passengers, who ate comfortably
in a full-service dining room and slept in

00:01:02.229 --> 00:01:03.400
private berths.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:24.380
And in 1936, the biggest and grandest airship
yet was built — the German LZ129, named

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:25.600
the Hindenburg.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:28.960
There she is, a fine example of simplicity
and economy of design.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.750
A triumph of lighter-than-aircraft, the likes
of which the world has never seen before.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:37.880
It was a monstrous feat of engineering, and
a symbol of Germany’s rising prominence in the

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.950
early years of the Nazi party.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.310
The world’s greatest lighter-than-air ship,
the Hindenburg!

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:46.850
That superliner of the sky made her first
trip to America from Germany in March, 1936.

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:52.280
That year, the Hindenburg made 17 intercontinental
round trips carrying passengers.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.590
It had a dining room, too.

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:55.730
And a reading room.

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:57.950
And a separate lounge.

00:01:57.950 --> 00:01:59.590
It even had a smoking room.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:04.670
A separately-ventilated space where passengers
could smoke under the supervision of the maitre’d,

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:06.700
who held the only lighter.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:11.260
Which brings us to a very important element
in this story – hydrogen.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:17.160
Hydrogen gas is lighter than air — and people
had used it for centuries to lift aircraft

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:18.569
like hot air balloons.

00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:24.329
It’s highly flammable but cheap and easy
to make – as opposed to its safer, non-flammable

00:02:24.329 --> 00:02:26.420
cousin, helium.

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:31.230
But the United States, who at this time produced
the rare gas almost exclusively, embargoed

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.500
its sale in 1925.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:38.430
Countries around the world developing lighter-than-air
programs had to rely on hydrogen — and their

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:40.370
airships were blowing up.

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:45.920
In the US, the hydrogen-powered Roma exploded
and killed 34 in 1922.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:51.390
In 1923, the French Navy airship Dixmude did
too – 52 dead.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:56.590
And in 1930, the UK ship R101 went down — 48
dead.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:58.209
But the Germans weren’t worried.

00:02:58.209 --> 00:03:03.650
By the time the Hindenburg embarked on its
first North American flight of 1937, not a

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.940
single German passenger airship had crashed.

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:19.909
On May 6, 1937, that’s not what they
did.

00:03:19.909 --> 00:03:22.219
The ship was 12 hours late.

00:03:22.219 --> 00:03:26.250
And what’s worse – the crew had good reason
to believe it was leaking hydrogen – the

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:30.480
back end was dipping toward the ground and
they couldn’t properly balance the ship.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.360
For three hours, the dirigible circled the
landing field at Lakehurst New Jersey, dumping

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:39.279
more water ballasts than ever before in vain efforts to level off.

00:03:39.279 --> 00:03:44.659
Plus, the air was charged with electrostatic
activity due to a recent thunderstorm – and

00:03:44.659 --> 00:03:46.849
another one was on the way.

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:51.469
That means they were trying to quickly land
an enormous hydrogen-filled airship, in electrically

00:03:51.469 --> 00:03:54.360
charged conditions, that was likely leaking
flammable gas.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.360
And… well...

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.920
“It’s burst into flames!

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.120
Get this Charlie, get this Charlie!

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:01.800
And it’s crashing!

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.300
It’s crashing, terrible!

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.640
Oh this is the worst of the worst catastrophes
in the world.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.760
Oh the humanity!”

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:12.340
When the Hindenburg caught fire, a crew of
reporters gathered for a routine photo opp

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:13.920
sprung into action.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.690
Photojournalist Sam Shere only had time for
a single exposure, but of all the press photos

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:22.139
taken that day, it’s this one that’s most
remembered.

00:04:22.139 --> 00:04:25.650
It somehow manages to perfectly frame the
entire disaster.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:30.770
The panicked crowd running in view of newsreel
cameras, with the hastily-dumped water ballasts

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:33.260
still visible in the background.

00:04:33.260 --> 00:04:37.780
The name “Hindenburg” - barely illuminated
by the bright flames lighting up the entire top of

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:38.460
the ship.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:44.060
And the faulty tail, now completely engulfed
— just moments before the flames burst out

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:47.120
of the nose and brought the ship crashing
to the ground.

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:55.150
The image of the Hindenburg burning was unlike
anything people had seen before – a disaster

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:58.150
photographed as it was taking place.

00:04:58.150 --> 00:05:04.530
In the end, out of 97 people on board, only
35 — plus one crewman on the ground — died.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:09.479
That means that about two-thirds of the people
inside of that actually escaped.

00:05:09.479 --> 00:05:11.430
But the numbers didn’t matter.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:15.370
Hydrogen airships never flew paid passengers
again after that day.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:20.650
The Hindenburg wasn’t the first
or even the deadliest passenger airship disaster.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:25.080
It was just the one that was caught on film
— and that’s why it was the last.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.590
The last episode of Darkroom was about Scott's
doomed expedition to the South Pole.

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:35.960
But there's some photos that we didn't include
- and one in particular - that I really want

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.720
to show you, and they're in this book.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:42.220
That's going to appear next week in Video
Lab, our YouTube membership program.

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:44.830
Just go to vox.com/join to learn more.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:46.810
And as always, thanks for watching.

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:49.150
I’m looking at you insane the entire time.

